## What happened

Apple removed DSA support from its security frameworks (Security.framework / CommonCrypto). In .NET 11, the runtime dropped the legacy OpenSSL-based DSA shim on macOS, so **`DSA.Create()` now throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`** on that platform. Linux and Windows still have native DSA support, so those builds are unaffected.

## How to fix it

Since you only need **verification** of legacy signatures (not new signing), you have two practical options:

### Option 1 — Use BouncyCastle (recommended)

Replace the built-in `DSA` class with **BouncyCastle's managed implementation**, which runs on all platforms with no native dependency:

```bash
dotnet add package BouncyCastle.Cryptography
```

```csharp
using Org.BouncyCastle.Crypto.Parameters;
using Org.BouncyCastle.Security;

bool VerifyDsaSignature(byte[] publicKeyDer, byte[] data, byte[] signature)
{
    var keyParam = (DsaPublicKeyParameters)PublicKeyFactory.CreateKey(publicKeyDer);
    var verifier = SignerUtilities.GetSigner("SHA1withDSA"); // or SHA256withDSA
    verifier.Init(forSigning: false, keyParam);
    verifier.BlockUpdate(data, 0, data.Length);
    return verifier.VerifySignature(signature);
}
```

### Option 2 — Runtime guard with conditional logic

If you want to keep using the built-in `DSA` on platforms that support it and only fall back on macOS:

```csharp
if (OperatingSystem.IsMacOS())
    return VerifyWithBouncyCastle(pubKey, data, sig);
else
    return VerifyWithBuiltinDsa(pubKey, data, sig);
```

### Long-term recommendation

DSA is a **legacy algorithm** (FIPS 186-4 deprecated it in favor of ECDSA). If you control the signing side, migrate to **ECDSA** or **Ed25519**, which are supported across all .NET platforms.
